1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,800
After a consultation, someone must turn
the note into orders and follow-ups.

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:09,600
It is minutes of repetitive work,
and where medication errors slip in.

3
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:15,200
This is the desk where the physician
and the agent do it together.

4
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:25,000
Rosa is fictitious, like all four cases.

5
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:29,500
She takes warfarin for atrial
fibrillation, and metoprolol.

6
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:34,200
In the note, the physician
dictated ibuprofen.

7
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:40,200
The agent reads the note and the record,
runs every drug through MEGI's checker,

8
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:45,000
and writes the plan
in one atomic operation.

9
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:53,000
What it says back is not a summary:

10
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:59,000
it is a receipt of what was actually
written, with every line counted.

11
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:05,500
Four lines. One safety block.
Physician review pending.

12
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:15,800
The agent proposed what the physician
dictated, and the action blocked it.

13
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:21,000
Ibuprofen with warfarin means bleeding.

14
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:27,200
By rejecting the line, the physician lets
the agent suggest for that line only.

15
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:34,000
The alternative stays out of the
prescription until the physician accepts.

16
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:45,500
Locked means untouchable.
That rule is not in the prompt:

17
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:50,500
it is in the action,
inside the transaction.

18
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:56,000
A visual selection grants nothing either.

19
00:01:56,300 --> 00:02:01,500
The physician authorises specific ids,
and the server revalidates them.

20
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:08,500
The physician can do any step by hand,
and the agent sees the same state.

21
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:16,000
Every change is an atomic version,
with its author and its time.

22
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:24,500
What was removed, what was added,
and where each line came from.

23
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:31,000
Drugs coming from the record arrive
unconfirmed, and they hold the approval.

24
00:02:31,300 --> 00:02:37,000
The agent cannot skip this.

25
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:44,500
Reconciliation belongs to the physician,
and the rule lives in the action.

26
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:51,500
The physician approves and confirms
the remaining alerts,

27
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:57,500
and the note leaves sealed to MEGI
as FHIR.

28
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:04,500
Clinical approval exists only here:
not over HTTP, MCP or the CLI.

29
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:11,500
The UI and the agent call the same
seventeen actions on the same records.

30
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:19,500
Every agent write carries the version it
read; if it arrives late, it re-reads.

31
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:25,500
The MEGI clinical backend is prior work
and was not modified.

32
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:30,200
The whole application was built
during the hackathon.

33
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:33,500
All patients are fictitious.
This is decision support:

34
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:37,000
the final approval is always
the physician's.
